python-libsbml — LGPL-2.1-or-later
----------------------------------

libSBML is copyrighted jointly by the California Institute of Technology,
the EMBL European Bioinformatics Institute, the University of Heidelberg and
others, and licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version
2.1 or any later version.

The LGPL permits a work to USE the library without that work becoming LGPL,
which is the whole point of the "Lesser" in its name. Its obligations —
notice, and the ability for a recipient to relink against a modified
libSBML — attach when you convey the library itself.

Terrium does not convey it. Nothing here bundles libSBML, and no build
artifact containing it is published: as of 2026-08-15 this repository has
exactly one CI workflow, `tests.yml`, and it publishes nothing. No Docker
image is pushed, no wheel is uploaded, no release artifact is produced.

THE TRIGGER THAT CHANGES THIS

  `.devcontainer/Dockerfile` runs `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt`, so
  an image built from it CONTAINS libSBML. Building that image locally is
  private use and unrestricted. **Publishing it — to Docker Hub, GHCR, or
  anywhere a third party can pull it — is conveying an LGPL library**, and
  the notice and relinking obligations attach at that moment.

  The same applies to any wheel, installer or bundle that vendors it.

  `scripts/check_release_artifacts.py` fails the build if a workflow that
  publishes is added while this NOTICE lacks the LGPL conveyance section.
  That guard exists so the obligation is not discovered after the fact.


stdpopsim — GPL-3.0-or-later, and why it is not installed by default
--------------------------------------------------------------------

stdpopsim (PopSim Consortium) is GPL-3.0-or-later. It was pinned in
`requirements.txt` until 2026-08-15, which meant `make setup` placed GPLv3
code into the default environment of a project whose LICENSE says
Apache-2.0.

Those licences are compatible in one direction only. Apache-2.0 code may be
taken into a GPLv3 work; GPLv3 code cannot be folded into a work
distributed under Apache-2.0 without the combination becoming GPLv3.

Nothing was being violated — Terrium never bundled stdpopsim, and running
two separately-installed packages together on your own machine is use, not
distribution. But a reader would have had to compare two licence files and
reason about their interaction to work out what they had, and this project's
central claim is that nothing should require that.

It now lives in `requirements-popgen.txt` and is opt-in. The code already
worked this way: `Tests/popgen_resolver.py` wraps `import stdpopsim` in
try/except ImportError and returns `found=False` with a search log saying
so. The manifest was the only thing claiming it was required.

See docs/adr/0061-gpl-out-of-the-default-install.md.


