THIRD-PARTY DATA IN THIS REPOSITORY
===================================

This file exists because Terrium redistributes data it did not create, and
that data carries obligations. It is separate from LICENSE, which covers
Terrium's own code.

Apache 2.0 §4(d) requires that this file travel with any redistribution, so
the attributions below reach downstream users by construction rather than by
someone remembering to copy them. That is a large part of why Apache 2.0 was
chosen over MIT — see the note at the end of LICENSE.


BRENDA — the enzyme database
----------------------------

Data obtained from BRENDA (https://www.brenda-enzymes.org/) is licensed
under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY
4.0):

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    https://www.brenda-enzymes.org/license.php

Copyright of the BRENDA content belongs to the BRENDA team at the Leibniz
Institute DSMZ — German Collection of Microorganisms and Cell Cultures
GmbH, Braunschweig, Germany.

WHERE BRENDA DATA APPEARS IN THIS REPOSITORY

  Tests/fixtures/brenda_*.html
      Eleven HTML fixtures containing rows captured live from BRENDA enzyme
      pages, used to test the parser without hitting the network.

  docs/literature-inventory.toml
      Golden-tuple entries recording BRENDA values and reference ids, e.g.
      the LDH/lactate Km (ref 740253) and the LDH/gossypol Ki (ref 711801).

  Tests/, Science-Agent-Pipeline/artifacts/api-server/src/
      BRENDA reference ids used as test expectations and citation fixtures.

MODIFICATIONS

CC BY 4.0 §3(a)(1)(B) requires that modifications be indicated. Terrium
does modify the data it extracts:

  * rows are filtered by organism and substrate
  * units are normalised and re-labelled
  * values whose source does not report assay pH and temperature are
    FLAGGED as incomplete under the STRENDA Guidelines rather than
    presented as verified
  * the HTML fixtures are trimmed excerpts of full BRENDA pages, not
    complete copies

The underlying measurements are unaltered. Terrium never edits a value.

NO WARRANTY

CC BY 4.0 §5: the Licensed Material is offered as-is and as-available, and
the licensor makes no representations or warranties of any kind concerning
it. Terrium restates that disclaimer here and extends it: a value resolved
through Terrium carries whatever uncertainty the original measurement
carried, and Terrium's provenance reporting does not make a measurement
more reliable than it was.

CITATION

If you use BRENDA data in scientific work, cite BRENDA's current
publication — see https://www.brenda-enzymes.org/references.php. Citing
Terrium is not a substitute for citing BRENDA.


CORE — queried live, and UNRESOLVED
----------------------------------

`Tests/core_fulltext.py` calls `https://api.core.ac.uk/v3/search/works` to
find open-access full text (ADR 0017). No CORE data is stored in this
repository.

CORE's Terms & Conditions (https://core.ac.uk/terms) distinguish their
ODC-By datasets, which may be used commercially with acknowledgement, from
**the CORE API**, which is not covered by that grant:

    While you need to obtain a licence to use other CORE datasets as well as
    the CORE API, many of our users are eligible for a free licence.

    However, you should contact us [...] if you are developing or intending
    to develop a product, service or software using CORE data which:
    might be monetised now or in the future OR is expected to serve only a
    restricted group of beneficiaries OR is intended for the benefit of
    everyone but related to the functionality provided by one of CORE's
    existing services, such as but not limited to an API, a recommender
    system, search, discovery system or analytical dashboard.

**Terrium meets all three conditions**, not one: it may be monetised (this
repository holds an incorporation checklist and a fundraising tracker), and
it uses the CORE API to provide literature search and discovery, which is
CORE's own listed example.

CORE say many such users are eligible for a **free** licence and ask to be
contacted regardless. That is a form to fill in, not a bill. The action is
to contact them, and CORE must be acknowledged in any case -- which this
paragraph does, and which the resolver should do in its provenance output
once a licence position is settled.


PubChem — queried live
----------------------

`Tests/enzyme_lookup.py` calls
`https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/<name>/synonyms/JSON`
to resolve compound identity, used by `buffer_identity.py` and
`form_mixture.py` to tell one substance from another (ADR 0028, 0035).

PubChem is produced by the National Center for Biotechnology Information,
U.S. National Library of Medicine. Like other NCBI databases it is in the
public domain within the United States and carries no licence condition.
NCBI asks that PUG REST clients stay under 5 requests per second and
identify themselves; that is a usage policy rather than a licence term, and
it is recorded here because exceeding it gets a client blocked.


KEGG — queried live, and UNRESOLVED
----------------------------------

Terrium makes live calls to `https://rest.kegg.jp/get/ec:<ec_number>` from
the server, in `resolve_substrate_from_kegg()`
(Science-Agent-Pipeline/artifacts/api-server/src/lib/science_agent_runner.py),
to fill in a substrate name when the caller did not supply one. No KEGG data
is stored in this repository; the value used is a substrate NAME, which is
then passed to BRENDA as a filter.

KEGG's own terms (https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/legal.html, last updated
1 October 2024) say:

    KEGG is an original database product, copyright Kanehisa Laboratories.

    Academic users may freely use the KEGG website [...] Academic users who
    utilize KEGG for providing services are requested to obtain an academic
    service provider license, which is included in the KEGG FTP academic
    subscription.

    Non-academic users must understand that KEGG is not a public database,
    nor is it a publicly funded database. Non-academic use of KEGG requires
    a commercial license.

**This is an open question, not a settled position.** Terrium calls KEGG
server-side while providing a service to its users, and this repository
contains an incorporation checklist, a cap table and a fundraising tracker.
On KEGG's stated terms that reads as either the academic service-provider
case or the commercial case, and both require a licence from Pathway
Solutions (https://www.pathway.jp/).

The inconsistency is worth naming plainly: SABIO-RK is recorded below as
deliberately NOT integrated because its terms are non-commercial only.
KEGG's terms are comparable, and KEGG was integrated anyway -- not by a
decision that weighed them, but because nobody read them. See
docs/LICENSING.md for the action item.

Nothing about this affects data already produced: KEGG contributes a
substrate name, not a number, and no KEGG content is redistributed here.


SABIO-RK — not currently used
-----------------------------

SABIO-RK (https://sabiork.h-its.org/) has been evaluated as a second
source and is NOT integrated. Its terms are non-commercial only, which is a
materially different obligation from BRENDA's CC BY 4.0. If it is ever
added, it must be behind an explicit opt-in and must not be committed as
fixtures.


NCBI Taxonomy
-------------

Organism lineages under `Tests/fixtures/taxonomy/` are captured from the
NCBI Taxonomy database, produced by the National Center for Biotechnology
Information, U.S. National Library of Medicine. NCBI databases are in the
public domain within the United States and carry no copyright restriction;
NCBI asks to be cited but does not require it as a licence condition.

Terrium uses these lineages at runtime to decide whether a kinetic value
measured in one organism may stand in for another (ADR 0024). The XML
wrapper around each fixture is reconstructed to match NCBI's eFetch shape;
the lineage content is captured. Each fixture says which is which in its
own header.


PubMed / NCBI
-------------

Terrium queries PubMed via NCBI E-utilities. PubMed records are largely US
Government works or publisher abstracts; Terrium stores reference
identifiers (PMIDs, DOIs) rather than article text.


Why this file exists at all
---------------------------

It was written on 2026-08-13, after Lisa Jeske of the BRENDA team replied
to a question about querying the database and pointed at the licence page.
Reading it revealed that Terrium was in breach on two counts:

  1. No CC BY attribution appeared anywhere in the repository, though
     BRENDA-derived material was committed and redistributed.

  2. LICENSE read "all rights reserved, no permission granted to copy,
     modify, or distribute" — which applied to the BRENDA fixtures too, and
     therefore told downstream recipients they may not copy material BRENDA
     had licensed them to copy. CC BY 4.0 §2(a)(5)(B) forbids exactly that.

The second point has since been resolved twice over: first by a carve-out
exempting BRENDA material, and then on 2026-08-13 by relicensing Terrium
itself to Apache 2.0, which imposes no restriction for the carve-out to
except. The carve-out remains in LICENSE anyway, because a reader who finds
BRENDA HTML in this repository should not have to reason about licence
interaction to learn which terms govern it.

CC BY 4.0 §6(b)(1) reinstates the licence automatically where a violation
is cured within 30 days of discovery. This file, the LICENSE carve-out, and
the per-fixture headers are that cure.

Recording the breach rather than quietly fixing it is deliberate: a project
whose central claim is that every number can be traced should not have an
untraceable moment in its own compliance history.
